WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.195
Capability discovery is the process by which an MCP client learns what a server offers.

00:00:05.195 --> 00:00:08.931
At a high level, the client can discover available tools, resources,

00:00:08.931 --> 00:00:12.667
or prompts and make those capabilities available to the host application.

00:00:12.667 --> 00:00:16.064
Discovery helps avoid hard-coding every integration into the AI application.

00:00:16.064 --> 00:00:18.102
It also creates a governance question:

00:00:18.102 --> 00:00:22.857
learning that a capability exists does not mean the capability should be trusted, enabled,

00:00:22.857 --> 00:00:24.555
or used in every context.

00:00:24.555 --> 00:00:27.612
A typical interaction flow starts with a user request.

00:00:27.612 --> 00:00:31.688
The AI application sends the relevant prompt and context to the model.

00:00:31.688 --> 00:00:35.764
The model may reason over the request and decide that additional context

00:00:35.764 --> 00:00:37.802
or a tool call would help.

00:00:37.802 --> 00:00:41.878
The host may present available capabilities, enforce policy, ask for user approval,

00:00:41.878 --> 00:00:44.595
or call a tool through the MCP client.

00:00:44.595 --> 00:00:47.992
The server executes the requested operation or returns resource content.

00:00:47.992 --> 00:00:52.407
The tool result then becomes part of the context for the final response.

00:00:52.407 --> 00:00:54.106
Traceability matters throughout that flow.

00:00:54.106 --> 00:00:58.182
Context and tool results should remain connected to their source so users

00:00:58.182 --> 00:01:00.899
and operators can tell where information came from.

00:01:00.899 --> 00:01:05.654
If a model summarizes a document, the application should know which document was used.

00:01:05.654 --> 00:01:07.692
If a tool returned a status,

00:01:07.692 --> 00:01:11.428
the application should know which server and downstream system produced it.

00:01:11.428 --> 00:01:14.485
Traceability supports user trust, troubleshooting, auditing, and incident response.

00:01:14.485 --> 00:01:16.863
Discovery should be paired with selection rules.

00:01:16.863 --> 00:01:20.939
A server may expose many capabilities, but a given user, workspace, tenant,

00:01:20.939 --> 00:01:24.335
or task should only see the capabilities that policy allows.

00:01:24.335 --> 00:01:26.034
Some tools may require approval.

00:01:26.034 --> 00:01:28.751
Some resources may be filtered by user identity.

00:01:28.751 --> 00:01:31.129
Some capabilities may be disabled in production.

00:01:31.129 --> 00:01:33.506
MCP gives teams a structured interaction model,

00:01:33.506 --> 00:01:37.582
but the host application still needs to decide what context and actions

00:01:37.582 --> 00:01:39.620
are appropriate for the current request.
